(window.__CLAIMS_ADD__||function(){})([{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Open enrollment for 2027 coverage opens on HealthCare.gov on 2026-11-01","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-1"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Enroll by Dec 15 for coverage that starts Jan 1","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-2"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The HealthCare.gov window closes Jan 15, 2027","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-3"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"This is the second season since the enhanced credits ended after 2025-12-31","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Illinois's final statewide weighted average for individual plans is +14.1% for 2027, against 28.8% last year","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-5"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CMS's national 2027 landscape (average benchmark premiums) has been published","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-6"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Treasury and the IRS proposed rule REG-101576-26 on the base credit, at 91 FR 65494, on 2026-10-15","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Comments on the IRS proposed rule are due Nov 30, 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-8"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A telephone hearing is set for Dec 8, 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-9"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Treasury expects about 3.7 million returns to include a repayment of excess advance credits in 2028, and gives no dollar figure","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-10"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The rule is expected to affect about 15.6 million Exchange enrollees, 13.5 million of them potentially eligible for the credit","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-11"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"H.R. 6500, signed 2026-09-02, funds the government to Dec 11, 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-12"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"H.R. 6500 contains nothing on the premium tax credit","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-13"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The state insurance commissioners' group (NAIC) sent a 10-15 letter on the broker moratorium","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-14"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The enhanced credits were created by the 2021 pandemic relief law (ARPA §9661, signed March 11, 2021) for 2021–22: 0% of income up to 150% of poverty, 8.5% at 400% and above, and no 400% cutoff","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The statute itself labelled them temporary","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The 2022 law (IRA §12001, Aug 16, 2022) extended them, including the rule for incomes above 400%, through tax years beginning before Jan 1, 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The enhanced credits ended by their own sunset date. No 2025 law cut them","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-18"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The 2025 tax-and-budget law is Pub. L. 119-21, approved July 4, 2025","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-19"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"§71305 removes the cap on paying back excess advance credits, for tax years beginning after 2025","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-20"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The repayment cap that was removed applied only below 400% of poverty; above it, repayment was already uncapped","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-21"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"§71302 ends the rule that let lawfully present immigrants below 100% of poverty (barred from Medicaid by status) claim the credit, from 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-22"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"§71301 limits the credit to \"eligible aliens\" among lawfully present people, from tax year 2027","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-23"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"§71304 denies the credit for plans picked during an income-based special enrollment period, from plan year 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-24"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"§71303 makes exchange pre-verification of eligibility a condition of a credit month, from tax year 2028","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-25"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The 2026 contribution table puts the lowest earners at \"2.10%–4.19% below 150% of poverty\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-26"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"For 2026 the top contribution is a flat 9.96% of income at 300–400% of poverty, and the table stops at 400%: no credit above it","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-27"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"For 2027 coverage (this open enrollment) the top contribution rises to 10.22%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-28"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"2026 coverage uses the 2025 poverty guideline: $15,650 for one person and $21,150 for two, so 400% is $62,600 and $84,600","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-29"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"2027 coverage uses the 2026 poverty guideline: $15,960 for one and $21,640 for two, so 400% is $63,840 and $86,560","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-30"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The out-of-pocket cap is $10,600 for an individual in 2026 and $12,000 in 2027","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-31"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"HHS's 2025 Marketplace Integrity rule (90 FR 27074, June 25, 2025) projected 725,000 to 1.8 million fewer enrollees in 2026 from its own provisions","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-32"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The 2027 Payment Notice is at \"91 FR 29256\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-33"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"HHS's own projection: average 2027 Marketplace enrollment 1.2 million to 2 million lower than an 18.881 million baseline","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-34"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"HHS expects the people who remain to cost more, because healthier people leave","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-35"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"\"HHS estimates two million people lose coverage in 2027 and five million by 2030\" (the attorneys general)","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-36"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"21 attorneys general and Pennsylvania's governor sued over the 2027 Payment Notice in the Northern District of California on July 31, 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-37"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A Maryland federal court (City of Columbus v. Kennedy, No. 25-2114) vacated nine provisions of the 2025 rule, including the $5 charge on re-enrollees and the shortened open-enrollment window, by an order of June 12 clarified June 16, 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-38"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A separate Maryland case, No. 26-2215, stayed eight groups of the 2027 Payment Notice's provisions on July 16, 2026, pending a ruling on the merits","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-39"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The Fourth Circuit argument in the 2025-rule appeal on Oct 30 is \"secondary only\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-40"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A Massachusetts federal court (California v. Kennedy, 2026-08-14) went the other way on the window: HHS won on the uniform open-enrollment rule for state exchanges, and the states won only on essential health benefits","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-41"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"\"A 43-day shutdown, Oct 1 to Nov 12, 2025\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-42"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The law that ended the shutdown (H.R. 5371) was signed Nov 12, 2025","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-43"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The credits were the central dispute of the shutdown","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-44"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Dec 11, 2025: the Senate rejected cloture on taking up S. 3385 (extend the enhanced credit), 51–48, with 60 needed","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-45"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The same day, cloture on S. 3386 (a health savings account alternative) also failed 51–48","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-46"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The House passed H.R. 1834, a three-year extension, 230–196 on 2026-01-08","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-47"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"H.R. 1834 is a clean date change through 2028, retroactive to 2026, with no income cap, minimum premium or HSA option","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-48"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The House vote was forced by a discharge petition (No. 10, on H.Res. 780), filed Nov 12, 2025, which reached its 218th signature on Dec 17, 2025","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-49"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"In the Senate, H.R. 1834 was placed on the calendar (No. 319) on Feb 10, 2026, with no vote since","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-50"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A bipartisan Senate framework set a cap at about 700% of poverty, a $5 minimum premium and an HSA option","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-51"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The Senate framework collapsed in early February over abortion-coverage language","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-52"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The lead negotiator said \"It's effectively over\"","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-53"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The White House announced $500 per person to nearly 1 million people in 30 states","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-54"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The refunds go to people who do not receive premium assistance, in 30 federal-exchange states (\"mainly above 400% of poverty\")","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-55"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The checks were to go out \"beginning in October 2026\"","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-56"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The $500 checks were actually mailed and received","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-57"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Measured: across all plan selections, the average monthly premium after credits rose 58%, from $113 to $178, from 2025 to 2026 open enrollment","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-58"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Premiums rose 114% \"for the same plan\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-59"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"KFF's Oct 6 update says payments \"increased by 114%\" to stay in the same plan","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-60"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The average sticker (pre-credit) premium went from $619 (2025) to $741 (2026). Among credit recipients the net premium went from $74 to $96, and the average credit from $550 to $650","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-61"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CMS's open-enrollment report gives the 2026 pre-credit average as $619","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-62"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The share of enrollees getting a credit fell from 92% to 87%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-63"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The gross benchmark (second-lowest silver, for a 40-year-old) averaged $497 a month in 2025 and $625 in 2026, up 26%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-64"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"On Urban's own population-weighted measure, the benchmark rose 21.7% in 2026 ($500 to $609)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-65"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The average deductible rose 37%, from $2,759 to $3,786; at 2025's mix of metal levels it would have risen only 6%, to $2,912","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-66"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Bronze rose from 30% (7.3 million) to 40% (9.2 million) of selections, and silver fell to 43% (from 57% in KFF's count, 56% in CMS's)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-67"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"43% of HealthCare.gov selectors chose HSA-eligible plans, against 2% in 2025, because every bronze and catastrophic plan became HSA-eligible","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-68"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The share in cost-sharing-reduction (extra-help silver) plans hit a record low of 37%; in HealthCare.gov states, the share of eligible people in one fell from 66% to 45%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-69"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"23.1 million people selected a plan for 2026, about 1.2 million (5%) fewer. These are sign-ups, not people covered","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-70"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Paid-up (effectuated) enrollment in February was 19.2 million, against 21.8 million in 2025, a 12% drop","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-71"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The baselines differ: HHS's analysts use 22.1 million for February 2025, CMS's monthly data 21.8 million","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-72"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"HHS's own count for February 2026 is also 19.2 million","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-73"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The share of sign-ups who paid and were covered in February fell from 90% to 83%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-74"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Paid enrollment fell 15% in HealthCare.gov states and 6% in states running their own exchanges","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-75"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Ohio and Oklahoma fell just over 32%, Arizona 30%, South Carolina 29%, Michigan 27%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-76"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The loss came at the payment step: South Carolina's sign-ups fell 7% but its paid enrollment 29%; Michigan's 6% against 27%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-77"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Texas sign-ups rose about 206,000 (5%) while paid enrollment fell about 146,000 (4%)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-78"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Mississippi had the lowest share of sign-ups who paid, 61%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-79"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Paid enrollment fell in every state except New Mexico, which grew 14%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-80"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Every state that used its own money to replace some of the lost help did better than the median state","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-81"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"New Mexico had the highest share of sign-ups who paid, 96%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-82"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The whole individual market, on and off the exchanges, fell from 25.2 million (June 2025) to 20.5 million (June 2026), 18%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-83"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"People at 400–500% of poverty were 3% of 2025 sign-ups but 27% of the drop (sign-ups down 44%, over 321,000); everyone above the cliff was 7% of sign-ups and 48% of the decline","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-84"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Young adults, 18 to 34, account for 46% of the sign-up decline (about 542,000)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-85"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Rural sign-ups fell 10%, against 4% elsewhere","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-86"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Enrollment \"could fall to 17.5 million\" in 2026","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-87"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBO projected 16.9 million average Marketplace enrollment for 2026","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-88"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The February numbers are early: returning subsidised enrollees get a three-month grace period, and full-year 2026 data arrive around summer 2027","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-89"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Urban projected 7.3 million fewer subsidised enrollees and 4.8 million more uninsured in 2026 if the credits expired","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-90"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"In Maryland, \"unpaid plans went from 2.9% to 13.3%\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-91"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"California fell 6%, Maryland 5%, Colorado 7%","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-92"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"New Mexico's legislature appropriated almost $40 million ($22.3 million plus $17.3 million) on top of a $50 million recurring line","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-93"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"New Mexico's premium assistance went from about $19.2 million a year (2025) to about $147.6 million (2026)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-94"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Federal credits still pay most of New Mexico's help: $494.4 million projected, against up to $150 million from the state","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-95"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"New Mexico's fund \"may be insolvent by FY28\"; the state health agency projects an $85.3 million shortfall in FY28, rising to $273.3 million by FY30","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-96"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The fund is fed by a premium surtax; HB4 raises the fund's share of it from 55% to 95% from September 2028","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-97"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"New Mexico's average net premium rose 6.8%, to $141 a month; with federal credits alone it would have gone from $152 to $299","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-98"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"About $2,000 of state money per New Mexico enrollee-year","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-99"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"California's state subsidy went from $190 million (2026) to $300 million (2027), covering incomes to 200% of poverty, more than 500,000 people (about 30% of enrollees)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-100"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"California had been receiving about $2 billion a year in enhanced credits, so its state money replaces only a small share","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-101"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Covered California's preliminary 2027 weighted average rate increase is 9.9%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-102"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Maryland replaces all of the lost help below 200% of poverty and part of it from 200% to 400%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-103"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Colorado pays up to $80 a month per person at 100–400% of poverty, about 40% of the lost help","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-104"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Washington set fixed amounts: $55 a month alongside federal credits, $250 without","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-105"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"New Jersey, Massachusetts, Connecticut, Vermont and New York already had their own subsidies before the lapse","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-106"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Census: 7.9% (26.7 million) uninsured for all of 2025, unchanged. 2025 is before the lapse","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-107"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The federal health survey's early release covers 2025 only: 11.6% of 18–64-year-olds and 8.3% (28.0 million) of all ages uninsured","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-108"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The only federal reading after the lapse, the Census \"current coverage\" table for 2026: 11.9% of adults 19–64 uninsured (23.45 million), ±0.3","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-109"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The same table a year earlier: 11.6% (23.0 million), ±0.3; all people 8.5%, ±0.2. The 0.3-point change sits inside the combined margins","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-110"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Each of the Census table's rates carries ±0.3","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-111"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The 2025 American Community Survey 1-year estimates arrive Oct 29; they cover 2025, so they are pre-lapse","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-112"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The ACS date slipped because of a Commerce Department disclosure order","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-113"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"KFF re-interviewed 1,117 people who had 2025 Marketplace coverage (Feb 12 – Mar 2, 2026, ±3.8): 9% now uninsured, 22% in other coverage, 69% re-enrolled","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-114"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"14% of former enrollees aged 18–29 are uninsured, against 7% of those 50 and over","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-115"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A large hospital chain (HCA) said more uninsured patients, \"primarily\" people who lost exchange coverage, cost it about $400 million of second-quarter pre-tax income, and put the 2026 hit at $1.0–1.2 billion","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-116"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The chain's chief executive: exchange volumes down 15%, uninsured up 15%, \"almost one for one\"","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-117"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"No other 2026 survey of the national uninsured rate exists (Gallup, Urban, Commonwealth)","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-118"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The federal health survey has no January–June 2026 report scheduled","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-119"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Across 276 insurers in all 50 states and DC, the median proposed 2027 increase is 15%; the 2026 finalised median was 20%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-120"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Rising health care prices remain the main driver: the median medical trend for 2027 is 10%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-121"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Insurers put the sicker-pool effect of the lapse at about 4 points of 2026 premiums and roughly 4 more in 2027","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-122"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"KFF's preliminary review (77 insurers, median 14%) dates from \"July 2027\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-123"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"New York approved 6.0% for individual plans, against 20.6% requested","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-124"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Connecticut approved 11.3%, against 16.2% requested","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-125"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Pennsylvania approved 16.0%, down from 17.1% requested","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-126"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Washington approved 22.2%, against 22.4% requested, and its regulator cites healthier people leaving","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-127"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The spread from New York's 6% to Washington's 22.2% reflects regulator power","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-128"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Cigna leaves the individual market in all 11 of its states for 2027, about 369,000 people","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-129"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"11 insurers have announced exits for 2027 (as of Oct 7), and six will enter new states","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-130"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Molina shrinks from about 14 states to 6 for 2027","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-131"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Molina's second-quarter Marketplace medical cost ratio was 88.9%, higher than expected, partly from a sicker member mix","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-132"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBO (Sept 18, 2025): making the enhanced credits permanent adds $349.8 billion to deficits over 2026–2035","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-133"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"…and 3.8 million more people insured in 2035 (3.6 million in 2030)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-134"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The gain by year, assuming enactment on 2025-09-30: 2.0 million (2026), 3.5 million (2027), then about 3.6–3.8 million","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-135"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Gross benchmark premiums would be 7.6% lower on average over 2026–2035, and 2.4% lower in 2026 under that enactment date","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-136"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A later enactment would cost less and cover fewer people in 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-137"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Repealing the 2025 law's Marketplace sections would add 2.9 million insured in 2035, at $272 billion","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-138"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBO's split of the permanent extension by coverage type (employer, Marketplace, Medicaid)","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-139"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"$350 billion divided by 3.8 million (about $92,000) is a cost per person","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-140"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBO (Jan 8, 2026): H.R. 1834, extending through 2028, adds $80.6 billion to deficits over 2026–2035, about $74.5 billion of it in FY2026–28","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-141"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"H.R. 1834 would add 0.1 million insured (2026), 3.0 million (2027), 4.0 million (2028) and 1.1 million (2029); the 2028 figure nets +6.2 million Marketplace, +0.4 million Medicaid/CHIP, −0.5 million off-exchange and −2.1 million employer","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-142"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Under H.R. 1834, 2.1 million fewer people would have job-based coverage in 2028","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-143"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Under H.R. 1834, benchmark premiums are unchanged in 2026, then 5.7%, 9.0% and 3.3% lower in 2027–29","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-144"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"\"About $35 billion a year\"","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-145"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"\"About $1,600 per enrollee a year\"","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-146"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBO (Dec 5, 2024): without a permanent extension the uninsured rise 2.2 million (2026), 3.7 million (2027) and 3.8 million on average over 2026–34, and gross benchmark premiums rise 4.3%, 7.7% and 7.9% on average","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-147"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBO: more employers stop offering coverage under a permanent extension than under a temporary one","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-148"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBO and JCT (July 2022, on CBO's May 2022 baseline): permanent credits raise the deficit $247.9 billion over 2023–2032 and premium tax credits $305.5 billion","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-149"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"$242.2 billion, about three-quarters of the added credits, goes to 4.8 million new enrollees a year, averaging $4,980 each","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-150"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Fewer than half of the new Marketplace enrollees are newly insured: the uninsured fall 2.2 million against 4.8 million more enrollees (46%)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-151"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The difference: 2.3 million fewer with job-based coverage and 0.5 million fewer off-exchange, partly offset by 0.2 million more on Medicaid/CHIP","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-152"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"People who move from job-based coverage would get an average $3,830 a year tax benefit","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-153"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Most of the new enrollees below 400% of poverty were already eligible and sign up because the help is bigger","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-154"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"In the 2022 letter, 29% of new-enrollee credit dollars go above 400% of poverty, and 48% of new enrollees (2.3 million of 4.8 million) are above it","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-155"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"About $11,000 of deficit per newly insured person-year","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-156"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBO's 2024 permanent-extension score: 3.8 million more insured on average over 2026–2034","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-157"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"\"31% of the total cost goes to people above 400% of poverty\" (a House committee's summary)","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-158"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The 2024 letter: a permanent extension brings 6.9 million more Marketplace enrollees but 3.5 million fewer with job-based coverage, and those who move get an average $4,350 tax benefit","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-159"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"86% of enhanced-credit spending in 2026 would go to enrollees earning under $150,000, and 94% under $200,000","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-160"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Capped versions would cost less: about $325 billion with a 600% limit, about $280 billion to 300% with a phase-up, about $175 billion at half strength","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-161"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Funding cost-sharing reductions (ending \"silver loading\") would cut the deficit $31 billion and add 300,000 uninsured through 2034","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-162"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The income-tax exclusion for employer health insurance is worth $226.2 billion in FY2025 and $240.4 billion in FY2026, so one year of it is roughly seven times the yearly average cost of a permanent extension","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-163"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"JCT counts income tax only, not payroll tax","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-164"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Treasury (2022 data): below 200% of poverty the Marketplace credit averaged $6,420 a person, against $1,650 for the employer exclusion (income plus payroll tax)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-165"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"At 800% of poverty and above it was $730 against $2,580, and that band holds 25% of people with employer coverage","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-166"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The employer benefit overtakes the average credit \"between 500% and 600% of poverty\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-167"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Across everyone: the employer exclusion averaged $2,200 and the credit $5,150","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-168"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Nearly 2 million enrollees have known incomes above 400% of poverty, about 1 million above 500%, and another 1 million have no income data","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-169"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"In 2025 selections, 724,961 people were at 400–500% and 911,808 above 500%, about 1.64 million (about 7%)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-170"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"About half of individual-market enrollees above 400% are aged 50–64","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-171"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"38% of adult individual-market enrollees above 400% are self-employed, against 7% of adults nationally","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-172"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"About half (48%) of adult individual-market enrollees are self-employed, small-business owners or small-business employees","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-173"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A 60-year-old single person at 401% of poverty ($62,757) pays from $4,469 more a year (New York) to $22,452 more (Wyoming); West Virginia $22,006, Alaska $19,636","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-174"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"For that 60-year-old, the payment at least doubles in 46 states plus DC, and triples in 19","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-175"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A couple, both 63, in West Virginia, just over 400% (under $85,000), faces a premium increase of over $50,000 a year","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-176"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A couple's cliff of about $43,900 (a Wyoming city's premium doubled)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-177"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"For a 60-year-old earning $65,000, bronze goes from 2% to 18% of income, and silver and gold from 8.5% to 24%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-178"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The 2027 couple: at $86,000 they pay at most about $8,800 a year for the benchmark plan; just over $86,560 they pay full price","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-179"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"KFF's pre-season examples: a 27-year-old earning $35,000 goes from $1,033 to $2,615 a year; a couple both 55 earning $85,000 would pay $20,792 at flat premiums","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-180"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A 60-year-old couple pays more than $2,000 a month in Pittsburgh","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-181"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"More than a quarter of farm and ranch managers buy in the individual market","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-182"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Peer-reviewed: in the Massachusetts exchange, linking subsidies to prices raised prices 1–6%, much more where competition was thin","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-183"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Less than half of ACA premium subsidies reach consumers, and a third of net spending is deadweight loss","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-184"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBO's model runs the other way: the lapse raises gross benchmark premiums, because healthier people leave","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-185"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Benchmark growth averaged 2.0% a year over 2021–25, then 21.7% in 2026","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-186"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"When cost-sharing payments to insurers stopped in 2017, CBO projected federal costs rising $6 billion (2018), $21 billion by 2020 and $26 billion by 2026","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-187"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A peer-reviewed estimate exists of how far the 2021–25 credits passed through to premiums or hospital prices","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-188"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"HHS's analysts estimate 5.6 million \"fraudulent or phantom\" enrollees in 2025","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-189"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"How the 5.6 million was built: observed enrollment, minus an estimated 6.1 million who moved in from other coverage, minus a 2019 baseline","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-190"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"HHS's analysts set the 2.9 million \"removed\" equal to the whole February-to-February drop (22.1 million to 19.2 million)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-191"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The 2.9 million is a modelled residual","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-192"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Most of CMS's 1.5 million were subsidy terminations, not coverage cancellations; about 250,000 were cancelled unauthorized enrollments in 2025","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-193"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"HHS's signals are proxies: 27.8% of $0-premium enrollees had cancelled by May 2026, and more than 80% of cancellations were broker-assisted; the cause of each cancellation is unknown","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-194"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Among people moved automatically from $0 plans into one with a premium, about 18% historically failed to pay and lost coverage, against about half in 2024–26","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-195"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The HHS report's authors are HHS and CMS officials (the exchange office's director and the chief economists)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-196"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Paragon (an advocacy think tank): about 6.2 million improper 2026 sign-ups, about 27% of all","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-197"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Paragon: up to $25 billion of improper subsidy payments in 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-198"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Researchers dispute what zero claims prove, which is Paragon's method","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-199"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Paragon's own series says improper sign-ups barely fell from 2025 to 2026, by about 250,000 (about 4%)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-200"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Paragon puts improper enrollment heavily in HealthCare.gov states: Florida (2.40 million), Texas (1.54 million), Georgia (0.54 million)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-201"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CMS's 2025 rule called its own 4.4 million (2024) \"erroneous or improper\" figure an upper bound","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-202"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"An Urban Institute economist, commenting in a personal capacity, found three flaws in the income-band method, all biasing it upward, while conceding some real improper enrollment, clearest in Florida","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-203"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The September rule's high end, $6.6 billion a year, rests on a zero-claims gap: 34% on-exchange against 23% off-exchange","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-204"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Footnote 29's comparison, 34% against 18–23%, also appears in the 2027 Payment Notice","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-205"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The high end's arithmetic: 1,364,681 enrollments (about 11% of 12,406,195) × 0.9 × $5,392 ≈ $6.6 billion","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-206"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"HHS itself says the high end may overstate","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-207"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The low end, $1.5 billion, comes from unauthorized enrollments","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-208"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"HHS says the low end's eight-month duration likely understates fraud","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-209"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The rule's own estimate of what the broker moratorium avoids is far smaller: about $48 million to $877 million a year","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-210"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"\"That spread ($6.6 billion against $1.5 billion) is the fraud dispute, in the government's own document\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-211"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"KFF's critique: CMS's no-claims file counts plan enrollments, not people, so switching plans and part-year spells inflate it; KFF still says fraud is real and the dispute is its scale","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-212"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"An insurer-commissioned actuary found short enrollment spans drive no-claims rates up (63% under four months, against 22% at eight months or more, 2023), conceded improper enrollment likely raised the rate, and did not measure how common it is","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-213"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBPP (advocacy): state exchanges that \"never experienced\" unauthorized enrollment also lost people (California about 20% of selections by Apr 6, Georgia 28% by Apr 17), so \"it's just fraud\" is improbable","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-214"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"In California, cancellations were concentrated among middle-income people who lost help; the lowest-income enrollees, shielded by state money, were the only group less likely to drop","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-215"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A Brookings economist called HHS's \"the whole decline was improper enrollees leaving\" \"not remotely credible\"","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-216"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"On Aug 31, 2026, CMS cancelled about 315,000 enrollments covering more than 760,000 people, calling them unauthorized","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-217"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CMS expects about $2.2 billion of advance credits to come back","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-218"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"How \"confirmed\" worked: CMS flagged about 1.2 million enrollments with four markers (no Social Security or immigration number, broker-assisted, $0 premium, no contact or claims), insurers reached out, and people who did not respond in 30 days were removed","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-219"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Some of the 760,000 have been reinstated, and a second set of 419,000 sign-ups is under review","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-220"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The same day, CMS paused new agent and broker registrations (not enrollments) for 2027, for those without a 2026 agreement, until Feb 1, 2027, on the federal exchanges only","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-221"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The interim final rule is 91 FR 60317, published 2026-09-23, effective 09-22, comments due 11-21","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-222"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CMS (2026-10-14): 189,000 people found to have duplicate Medicare, Medicaid or CHIP coverage, or to be deceased, had their subsidies removed","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-223"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"…projected to save about $1.5 billion over the next year","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-224"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"GAO's covert test, plan year 2024: all 4 fictitious applicants got subsidised coverage (about $2,350 a month in credits combined)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-225"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Plan year 2025: the Marketplace initially approved 19 of 20 fictitious applicants","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-226"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"18 of the 20 were still covered as of September 2025, with over $10,000 a month in credits combined","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-227"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"\"23 of 24 fake applicants were approved\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-228"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"GAO's fake applicants drew \"$12,300 a month\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-229"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"GAO says its covert test illustrates a vulnerability and cannot be generalised to how common fraud is","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-230"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"GAO found at least 30,000 (2023) and at least 160,000 (2024) applications with likely unauthorized broker changes","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-231"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Over 58,000 credit-receiving Social Security numbers matched death records in 2023, with over $94 million paid in a subset","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-232"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"GAO calls its results preliminary, and its final 2025 report has not been published","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-233"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Complaints of confirmed unauthorized enrollments and plan switches grew more than fourfold from 2023 to 2025","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-234"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"GAO counted 299,604 such complaints","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-235"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A Florida scheme sought over $233 million in subsidies, of which the government paid at least $180 million","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-236"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The brokerage president and a marketing company's chief executive each got 20 years and $180.6 million restitution after a November 2025 conviction; DOJ says the scheme \"preyed on tens of thousands\"","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-237"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A brokerage agreed to plead guilty ($27.6 million restitution) and its former parent agreed to pay $107 million under the False Claims Act, over $141.5 million in unwarranted subsidies","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-238"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"In Massachusetts (2016–17), moving from $0 to under $10 a month cut enrollment 14%, mostly through non-payment at the start","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-239"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A Brookings estimate: ending $0 premiums would cost about 960,000 people coverage and save about $7.1 billion","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-240"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A Senate bill (S. 3380) writes a $5 minimum premium for every subsidised enrollee, plus photo ID","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-241"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Senate drafts would deposit $1,000–$1,500 into an HSA for bronze buyers to 700% of poverty, or cap credits at 700% with stepped percentages","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-242"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Reinsurance waivers cut sticker premiums (Alaska 26–37%) but mostly help unsubsidised buyers; 2026 federal pass-through about $3.76 billion across 18 states","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-243"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Two large insurers' 2026 books: one's Marketplace membership fell about 40%, another's grew 46%","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-244"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Individual insurers' filings: one Pennsylvania insurer added a 3.0% fraudulent-claims load; another says its rates would be about 2% lower with the credits restored","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-245"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A single adult earning $20,000 is about 128% of poverty for 2026 coverage, inside the flat 2.10% band, so pays at most about $420 a year (about $35 a month) for the benchmark plan","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-246"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"For 2027 coverage the bottom band is 2.15% below 133% of poverty, so the same $20,000 single adult (about 125% of the 2026 guideline) pays at most about $430 a year","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-247"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The $20,000 example also works for a household of two","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-248"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"As of October 2026, 10 states have not adopted the Medicaid expansion (Alabama, Florida, Georgia, Kansas, Mississippi, South Carolina, Tennessee, Texas, Wisconsin, Wyoming); 41 including DC have","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-249"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"In an expansion state a single adult at $20,000 qualifies for Medicaid, and a month eligible for Medicaid earns no premium tax credit; so the 2.10% bill applies to that person only in a non-expansion state (or during a green-card holder's five-year wait)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-250"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CMS's own rule: the subsidised Marketplace starts at 138% of poverty in expansion states and at 100% in non-expansion states; below 100% in a non-expansion state is the coverage gap","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-251"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"2026 open enrollment: 7.38 million plan selections were at 100–138% of poverty; 6.54 million of them (about 89%) were in the 10 non-expansion states, 4.71 million in Florida and Texas alone","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-252"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"54% of all 23.1 million 2026 selections were in non-expansion states; about 52% of their selections were at 100–138% of poverty, against about 8% in expansion states","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-253"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The premium tax credit is paid from the Treasury under a standing appropriation for refundable credits that names section 36B; no yearly vote, no premium pool","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-254"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"In the budget the credit is lower revenue (the part that cancels tax owed) plus federal spending (the part paid beyond it)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-255"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The advance credit is paid monthly by the government straight to the insurer, lowering the enrollee's bill","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-256"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A deficit is paid for by federal borrowing (selling Treasury securities), so an unoffset extension is borrowed money, repaid later with interest","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-257"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"162.75 million individual income-tax returns were filed in fiscal 2025","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-258"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"There were 137.1 million US households in 2025 (Census CPS ASEC, households as of March 2026)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-259"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A permanent extension (about $35 billion a year) is about $255 per household, or about $215 per tax return, a year on average","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-260"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The three-year bill (H.R. 1834) is about $180 per household, or about $155 per tax return, a year on average in fiscal 2026–28; its costliest year (fiscal 2027) about $230 per household","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-261"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The income-tax exclusion for job-based insurance ($240.4 billion, fiscal 2026) is about $1,750 per household, or about $1,480 per tax return, a year on average","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-262"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Of the permanent extension's $350 billion, about $296 billion is extra spending and about $54 billion lower revenue","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-263"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Job-based coverage is the most common kind: 53.5% of everyone (180.7 million people) had it at some point in 2025","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-264"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"About 14.3 million people (about 4.2%) had Marketplace coverage at some point in 2025, by Census's survey","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-265"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Census's \"direct-purchase\" (10.5%) includes Marketplace plans plus plans bought straight from an insurer, so it is not \"the Marketplace\"","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-266"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"About 64% of adults 19 to 64 had job-based coverage in 2025 (125.3 million of 196.8 million)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-267"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBO's reason for lower gross premiums with an extension: the people enrolling would be healthier on average; its figures are before the credit","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-268"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"For a bill passed now, 2026 sticker prices don't move because they are already set; 2027 is the first year CBO's lower gross premium (5.7%) applies","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-269"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"An insurer pools all its individual-market enrollees in a state, on and off the exchange, in one risk pool, so off-exchange buyers share the Marketplace's price","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-270"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The credit is the benchmark price minus a set share of income, so a subsidised buyer of the benchmark plan is shielded from a gross price change and someone with no credit pays it in full","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-271"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A 40-year-old in Indianapolis earning $65,000 paid $316 a month for one silver plan in 2025 with the enhanced credit, $477 in 2026, and $546 in 2027 if the filed rates are approved","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-272"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"California's 2027 rates rise 9.9% on average, yet 60% of enrollees see no rise in what they pay","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-273"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The law records a 2010 congressional finding that uncompensated care for the uninsured raises family premiums by over $1,000 a year","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-274"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Each additional uninsured person costs local hospitals about $900 a year in uncompensated care, and hospitals cannot pass all of it to privately insured patients","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-275"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Projected for this lapse: uncompensated care rises $7.7 billion in 2026 (12%); slightly over half paid by providers, 30% federal, 19% state and local","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-276"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The lapse measurably raised one large hospital chain's uninsured volume (log 116); whether such losses reach job-based premiums is not measured for 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-277"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Hospitals raise commercial prices to offset low government prices (the cost-shift claim)","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-278"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Cost shifting from public payers or the uninsured is large and pervasive","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-279"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBO assumes employers that stop offering coverage keep workers' total pay about the same, so wages rise","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-280"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBO and JCT (2018): appropriating cost-sharing reduction (CSR) payments reduces the deficit, because insurers stop loading the CSR cost onto silver premiums, which shrinks the credits pegged to them","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-281"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBO on House-passed H.R. 1 §44202 (CSR funding): outlays down about $30.8 billion and revenue up about $2.8 billion, FY2025–34","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-282"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"That CSR funding was not enacted; silver loading continues","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-283"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"If CSRs are funded, subsidised silver buyers see little change while subsidised bronze and gold buyers likely pay more","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-284"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Funding CSRs lowers premiums about 11% (sponsors) or 12% (press reports of CBO)","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-285"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"S. 3386, the Health Care Freedom for Patients Act of 2025, was introduced Dec 8, 2025 by the Senate Finance chair with the HELP chair and placed on the calendar","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-286"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"S. 3386 pays $1,000 a year (ages 18–49) or $1,500 (50–64) into an HSA for months in 2026 or 2027 on a bronze or catastrophic exchange plan, at or below 700% of poverty; it does not extend the enhanced credit","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-287"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"S. 3386's HSA payments are capped at $10 billion a year for FY2026 and FY2027","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-288"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"S. 3386 also funds CSRs from plan year 2027, so it is a CSR-funding bill too","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-289"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"CBO has scored S. 3386","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-290"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Only about $1 of every $2 in credits reaches consumers","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-291"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The 34 / 38 / 28 cents split rests on one working paper's model of the original credits in 2017, not on the enhanced credits","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-292"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The paper's model: about $20.5 billion of net spending yields about $6.9 billion to consumers, $7.9 billion to insurers and a $5.7 billion deadweight loss (2017, HealthCare.gov states, about 9 million enrollees)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-293"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Across regions, half the variation in private insurance spending is price; monopoly hospitals charge about 12% more than those with four or more rivals","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-294"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Insurers' 2027 Marketplace filings cite provider unit-cost increases from contract talks among their drivers","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-295"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The ACA says the only health plans the government may offer Members of Congress and their office staff are ACA-created or exchange plans","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-296"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Since January 1, 2014, Members and designated staff buy through DC Health Link's small-business (SHOP) exchange to keep an employer contribution, chosen by OPM because Congress sits in Washington","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-297"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The government's share for Members and staff is the ordinary federal-employee formula: 72% of the weighted average FEHB premium, capped at 75% of the plan's premium","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-298"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"\"Congress exempted itself from Obamacare\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-299"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"\"Congress gets the same Obamacare subsidy as you\" or \"gets its insurance free\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-300"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"For 2026, 20 states and DC ran their own exchange platforms and 30 states used HealthCare.gov","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-301"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"For 2027, 21 states plus DC are fully state-based (Oregon is new), two state-based exchanges use the federal platform (Arkansas, Oklahoma), and 27 are federally run","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-302"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"Kentucky's kynect, Covered California and Pennsylvania's Pennie are those states' ACA marketplaces, and the federal credit is available through them","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-303"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The federal premium tax credit is written for plans bought through a state-established exchange","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-304"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"A KFF poll measures how many people don't know their plan is an ACA plan","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-305"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"About a third of Americans didn't know the ACA and Obamacare are the same","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-306"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The middle bands: for 2027 coverage 8.66–10.22% of income at 250–300% of poverty (2026: 8.44–9.96%); under the 2021–25 schedule 4.0–6.0% at 250–300%, 6.0–8.5% at 300–400%, and 8.5% at 400% and higher","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-307"},{"s":"ep42-help-paying-your-premium","n":42,"c":"The income ladder, one adult, 2027 coverage (2026 guideline $15,960): $45,000 is about 282% of poverty, at most about $4,350 a year for the benchmark plan under the law now against about $2,375 under the 2021–25 schedule; $64,000 is about 401%, no credit now against at most $5,440; $86,000 (about 539%) and $130,000 (about 815%) get no credit now, and under the 2021–25 schedule only if the benchmark costs more than $7,310 or $11,050 a year; 700% of poverty is about $111,700","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-308"}]);